ded to force a matching of one color, disproving a conjecture of Barát, Gyárfás and Tóth.\n\nhexagon:2610.00329v1\n\nmath.CO — Combinatorics\n\n### Counterexamples to the multicolor nonnested matching conjecture\n\nContributed by Pedro Machado Manhães de Castro\n\nWe show that excluding nested pairs can increase the number of vertices needed to force a matching of one color, disproving a conjecture of Barát, Gyárfás and Tóth. A matching consists of edges with no shared endpoint. In a fixed vertex order, two edges are nested if both endpoints of one lie between those of the other. For $r$r colors and a target of $k$k edges, the classical theorem guarantees a matching of one color on $(r-1)(k-1)+2k$(r−1)(k−1)+2 k vertices when every pair is joined by an edge. For every $r,k\\ge3$r,k≥3, we construct a coloring at this very threshold in which every such matching contains a nested pair. Thus the nonnested threshold is larger by at least one vertex. Already with three colors, ten vertices force three disjoint edges of one color, while our coloring makes every such triple contain a nested pair. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI Mathematics — Searchable Preprint Catalogue (https://sites.pitt.edu/~hajlasz/OpenAI_Math_Catalogue.html)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This gives a negative resolution of the periodic tiling conjecture in dimension three. ... It therefore gives a counterexample to Borsuk's conjecture in dimension nine. ... The formalization constructs arbitrarily large finite simple counterexamples with independence number at most two.\n## 07 Combinatorics\n\n50 papers\n\nFamily 155·26 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n### A translational tile with no fully periodic tiling in dimension three\n\ntilingsaperiodicity\n\nRead PDF ↗GitHub source ↗Lean scope ↗\n\nAbstract & Lean details\n\nRepository abstract\n\nWe construct a finite translational tile in ℤ^{3} that admits tilings but no fully periodic tiling. Its unit-cube thickening has the same property in ℝ^{3}, even when arbitrary real translations are allowed. This gives a negative resolution of the periodic tiling conjecture in dimension three.\n\nResult family 155: A counterexample to periodic tiling in dimension three\n\nThe main result is listed as formalized in Lean.\n\nThis manuscript appears in the repository’s main-result formalization catalogue. The scope below identifies the covered statements and any limitations.\n\nThe periodic-tiling question asks whether a finite tile that tiles a lattice must admit a periodic tiling. The formalized counterexample is a finite tile in $\\mathbb Z^3$ that tiles but has no complement invariant under a finite-index subgroup. Its unit-cube thickening also tiles $\\mathbb R^3$ almost everywhere but admits no fully periodic tiling, even with arbitrary real translation vectors. The result also establishes that dimension three is the least lattice dimension where this failure occurs.\n\nRead this scope note on GitHub ↗\n\nPeriodicTilingThree.lean\n\nFamily 156·18 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n### A nine-dimensional counterexample to Borsuk's covering assertion\n\nBorsuk problemdiscrete geometry\n\nRead PDF ↗GitHub source ↗Lean scope ↗\n\nAbstract & Lean details\n\nRepository abstract\n\nThe compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors on ℝ^{4}, with the Frobenius metric, cannot be covered by ten sets of strictly smaller diameter. It therefore gives a counterexample to Borsuk's conjecture in dimension nine.\n\nResult family 156: Borsuk's conjecture fails in dimension nine\n\nThe main result is listed as formalized in Lean.\n\nThis manuscript appears in the repository’s main-result formalization catalogue. The scope below identifies the covered statements and any limitations.\n\nBorsuk's conjecture predicts that every bounded subset of $\\mathbb R^d$ can be covered by $d+1$ sets of strictly smaller diameter. The formalized counterexample is the compact set of rank-one orthogonal projectors onto lines in $\\mathbb R^4$, with the Frobenius metric. It lies in the nine-dimensional affine space of trace-one symmetric matrices, has diameter $\\sqrt2$, and cannot be covered by ten arbitrary sets of smaller diameter.\n\nRead this scope note on GitHub ↗\n\nBorsukNine.lean\n\nFamily 157·104 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n### A counterexample to Hadwiger's conjecture\n\ngraph coloringgraph minors\n\nRead PDF ↗GitHub source ↗\n\nAbstract & Lean details\n\nRepository abstract\n\nWe disprove Hadwiger's conjecture by constructing arbitrarily large graphs whose chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. The examples have independence number at most two, and even their ordinary fractional chromatic number exceeds their Hadwiger number. Thus they also disprove the fractional-coloring weakening discussed by Reed and Seymour.\n\nResult family 157: Graph coloring, clique minors, and Colin de Verdière invariants\n\nThe main result is listed as formalized in Lean.\n\nThis manuscript appears in the repository’s formalization catalogue. The scope below identifies the covered statements and limitations.\n\nHadwiger's conjecture predicts $\\chi(G)\\le h(G)$ for every finite graph, where $h(G)$ is the largest clique-minor order. The formalization constructs arbitrarily large finite simple counterexamples with independence number at most two. For a graph on $m$ vertices it proves $h(G)<26m/75+2/3<m/2\\le\\chi(G)$. This disproves the ordinary chromatic form. The paper's fractional-chromatic strengthening and the Colin de Verdière consequence are outside this selected statement.\n\nThe Linear List Hadwiger conjecture asks for a universal linear bound on list chromatic number in terms of clique-minor size. The formalization proves that there is one integer $C\\ge1$ such that every finite nonempty simple graph $G$ satisfies $\\chi_{\\mathrm{list}}(G)\\le C h(G)$, where $h(G)$ is the largest order of a clique minor. The constant is independent of the graph.\n\nRead this scope note on GitHub ↗\n\nListHadwiger.leanHadwigerCounterexample.lean\n\nFamily 157·131 pages\n\nLean: no formalization listed\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-09-17 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-09-17)\nciteturn7search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; We also give smaller counterexamples certified by exact integer data. ... It combines an equality analysis of Roberson's bound on the projective packing number in terms of Schrijver's theta with a structural argument inspired by Erdős-Ko-Rado theory. ... This proves a conjecture of Chen, Yang, and Zhang and, by Brändén's preservation theorem, settles the infinite log-concavity conjecture of Boros and Moll.\n\n2026-09-17\n\nJulius A. Zeiss\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA counterexample to the quantum Hedetniemi conjecture\n\nGodsil, Roberson, Šámal and Severini conjectured that the quantum chromatic number of the categorical product of two graphs equals the minimum of the quantum chromatic numbers of the factors. We disprove this conjecture: we construct explicit finite graphs $G,H$ with \\[ χ(G\\times H) \\leq 1538 < 1539 = \\min(χ_q(G),χ_q(H)).\\]The graphs are obtained from Zhu's counterexample to Hedetniemi's conjecture by using a base graph for which the Lovász theta number of the complement, and not only the fractional chromatic number, is large. The lower bound for the first factor is the theta bound. For the second factor we adapt Zhu's argument to projections that do not commute: the step that fixes the colors of a clique is replaced by identities between operators. Both lower bounds hold for colorings by projections in an arbitrary nonzero unital $C^*$-algebra. Hence the conjecture also fails for the spatial, approximate, commuting-operator and $C^*$-algebraic variants of the quantum chromatic number. We also give smaller counterexamples certified by exact integer data. The graph constructions, the certificates and the counterexample statements in the projective formulation are formalized in Lean~4.\n\n2026-09-17\n\nLorenzo Ciardo, Iris Hebbeker, Gideo Joubert, Jana Kreiß, Antoine Mottet\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nSchrijver-Delsarte rigidity in association schemes and undecidability of quantum graph homomorphism\n\nWe prove RE-completeness of the quantum homomorphism problem parameterised by families of graphs derived from the classic metric association schemes. These include Kneser graphs, $q$-Kneser graphs, and the complements of Johnson, Grassmann, and Hamming graphs. Our proof develops a spectral method for establishing non-contextuality of quantum polymorphisms. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe density Turán problem for some 3-uniform unihypercyclic linear hypergraphs. An efficient testing algorithm (https://annals-csis.org/Volume_5/pliks/260.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.1 years ago; according to the second conditional instruction. ... matching polynomial and the inhomogeneous density Turán ... between the clusters of a blow-up graph of the triangle - a\nProof. Let H\n=\n(V, E) be a weighted 3-uniform linear\nunihypercyclic hypergraph with hypercycle C3. Assume that\n|E(H)| = n. To prove this theorem we use induction on the\nnumber of hyperedges of the hypergraph H.\nIf this hypergraph consists of 3 hyperedges (i.e., H is\nisomorphic to C3, say with E(H) = {abc, cde, efa}), then\nLemma 1. (Bondy, et al. [6]) Let α, β, γ be the edge densities\nbetween the clusters of a blow-up graph of the triangle - a\ncycle C3. If\nFH(re, t) = 1 −t(rabc + rcde + refa)\nαβ + γ > 1, βγ + α > 1, γα + β > 1,\nthen the blow-up graph contains a triangle as a transversal.\nIII. CONCLUSION\nand the condition FH(re, t) = 0 means that t is a root of this\nmultivariate matchi